From Chromium (https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src, BSD-3-Clause, Copyright The Chromium
Authors), commit 564344ab9eb89b305366e60b4b4f8ab2f70e791c, "Guard XKB state accesses in
XkbKeyboardLayoutEngine" (https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8378881,
refs/heads/main@{#1718872}), landed after Chromium 156. Its change to
xkb_keyboard_layout_engine.cc, unchanged; its test sits after one 156 doesn't have, so core's
xkb_no_keymap_unittest.cc checks the same two calls instead. Drop it once the pin has the commit.

On Wayland the seat's keyboard can come with no keymap, as fcitx5's virtual one does while it's
the seat's only keyboard, and the modifiers it sends then crashed the engine in
xkb_state_update_mask (#1138). UpdateModifiers and GetDomCodeByKeysym now return nothing while
there's no XKB state, as XkbLookup already does.

--- a/ui/events/ozone/layout/xkb/xkb_keyboard_layout_engine.cc
+++ b/ui/events/ozone/layout/xkb/xkb_keyboard_layout_engine.cc
@@ -966,6 +966,14 @@ int XkbKeyboardLayoutEngine::UpdateModifiers(uint32_t depressed,
                                              uint32_t latched,
                                              uint32_t locked,
                                              uint32_t group) {
+  if (!xkb_state_) {
+    // The first XKB keymap may arrive after GTK IM events that depend on
+    // modifier state (e.g. fcitx5 firing on first input activation).
+    // Guard like XkbLookup does to avoid xkb_state_update_mask(NULL).
+    LOG(ERROR) << "No current XKB state";
+    return 0;
+  }
+
   auto* state = xkb_state_.get();
